    <!-- Charts help dialog. Mode names (%1$s, %2$s) come from charts_mode_* and
         charts_axis_* so the help always matches the buttons. -->
    <string name="charts_help_title">Reading the charts</string>
    <string name="charts_help_dismiss">Got it</string>
    <string name="charts_help_intro">Two toggles change how your numbers are drawn. Nothing you pick here changes your data - only how the chart looks.</string>
    <string name="charts_help_series_title">Series: %1$s vs %2$s</string>
    <string name="charts_help_raw_body">Plots each day exactly as you logged it. Use it to see precise day-to-day changes - though it can look jumpy.</string>
    <plurals name="charts_help_rolling_body">
        <item quantity="one">Replaces each day with the average over the %1$d day ending on it. This smooths out one-off spikes so the overall trend - whether your moods are drifting up or down - is easier to see.</item>
        <item quantity="other">Replaces each day with the average over the %1$d days ending on it. This smooths out one-off spikes so the overall trend - whether your moods are drifting up or down - is easier to see.</item>
    </plurals>
    <string name="charts_help_axis_title">Y axis: %1$s vs %2$s</string>
    <string name="charts_help_autofit_body">Zooms the vertical axis to just the range your data actually covers. Small movements become easy to see because the chart fills the space - but the line isn\'t measured against the scale\'s full range. The labels down the left are your logged values; when more than one scale is plotted they all share that one axis.</string>
    <string name="charts_help_absolute_body" formatted="false">Shows each line against its scale\'s full min-to-max range. Movements look smaller, but different scales line up, so you can compare one against another on the same chart. Scales marked \"lower is better\" are drawn flipped here, so better always points up. The labels down the left read as percentages because each line is measured against its own range: 100% is the top of that range, or the bottom for a scale marked \"lower is better\".</string>

